      "side": 1,
      "message": "Darren, James, Jerome: Although the changes to `vpx_img_set_rect()` fixed the invalid plane pointers when `img` is flipped, the semantics for setting a view rect on a flipped image is incorrect or at least controversial. I will explain with an example.\n\nConsider a plane of size 2x2 with the following sample values:\n\n```\n11 12\n21 22\n```\n\nSuppose we flip the plane, and we actually move the two rows in memory. Then the plane becomes:\n\n```\n21 22\n11 12\n```\n\nNow, we set the view rect (0, 0, 1, 1) on the flipped plane to extract only the first sample of the flipped plane.  We should get:\n\n```\n21\n```\n\n---\n\nIn contrast, the current `vpx_img_set_rect()` function first sets the view rect (0, 0, 1, 1) on the orignal plane and extracts the first sample of the original plane:\n\n```\n11\n```\n\nThen it flips the view rect:\n\n```\n11\n```\n\n---\n\nI hope I have given a convincing argument that the current behavior of `vpx_img_set_rect()` is incorrect, or at least controversial.\n\nSince I found no evidence that `vpx_img_flip()` is used outside its own unit tests, it is not worth our time trying to correct `vpx_img_set_rect()`. So I suggest reverting this CL and just having `vpx_img_set_rect()` return -1 if `img` is flipped.\n\n---\n\nAnother problem with this CL is that it introduced explicit handling of a zero height, at line 260. Since there is also no evidence that `vpx_image_t` was intended to support a zero width or zero height, we should not spend time trying to handle a zero width or zero height properly. We can let a zero width or zero height be \"undefined behavior\", or treat them as invalid.",
      "revId": "01a78fbc3b25c6a9eb227973bd591e9092d6daa1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    }
